nature skill at diplomacy beats skill at tactics, you may as well not
submerge the game in tactics. 

>Twilight Imperium is also a very nice big game with both strategy,
>diplomacy and a touch of politics.

Twiglet suffers from the problem that in a typical big game one or two
players will get into a position where they can't really win but
abandoning the game will have too big a kingmaker effect, and they still
have ages of slugging it out ahead.

Republic of Rome has a very ingenious solution to that; if the players
don't collaborate enough, the Republic will fall and everyone will lose.
Players in strong positions obviously have more of an incentive to prop up
the Republic, so it's hard to maintain a very strong position and
relatively easy for a player in a weak position to make a comeback (but a
strong position is still better than a weak one - the catchup isn't
absurd.)
